/** \page getting_started Getting Started

WebSocket++ code is available on GitHub at https://github.com/GreenLightning/websocketpp2
The project website lives at https://greenlightning.github.io/websocketpp2/

The git repository is organized into several directories:

- **docs**: This documentation
- **examples**: Example programs that demonstrate how to build basic versions of some commonly used patterns for WebSocket clients and servers.
- **test**: Unit tests that confirm that the code you have works properly and help detect platform specific issues.
- **tutorials**: Detailed walkthroughs of a select set of the example programs.
- **websocketpp**: All of the library code and default configuration files.

WebSocket++ is a header only library. You can start using it by including the websocketpp source directory in your project's include path and including the appropriate WebSocket++ headers in your program. You may also need to include and/or link to appropriate Boost/system libraries. TODO: More information: Building a program with WebSocket++, Walkthroughs of the example programs

WebSocket++ includes scripts requiring CMake 3.18 or later for building the examples and unit tests. CMake is not needed unless you want to build tests or examples in an automated fashion.

To build the examples and tests with standalone Asio instead of Boost.Asio, pass `-DASIO_STANDALONE=ON` to CMake (and `-DASIO_ROOT=<path>` if the Asio headers are not in a default location).

__Bugs and issues__ should be posted to the project GitHub issues queue: https://github.com/GreenLightning/websocketpp2/issues.

__Pull requests__ on GitHub are welcome. Please make them against the `master` branch. Every pull request is built and tested with GCC, Clang and MSVC, with standalone Asio, and with the oldest supported Boost and Asio versions.

WebSocket++ was written by Peter Thorson. This fork continues the original (now mostly inactive) project at https://github.com/zaphoyd/websocketpp.

*/

